nted these are still cartoon rules  but the tone of everything everywhere  all at once doesn't demand literal sense  what's important is that evelyn uses the  absurdity and chaos of the multiverse to  create joy rather than pain  even after fighting a dozen people to  prevent jobu from diving into the  everything bagel the divide between  evelyn and her daughter is not entirely  mended joy doesn't feel she can trust  that things will really get better with  her mother in the long run their  relationship is punctuated by intense  moments of drama that caused joy a great  deal of stress and pain and she still  thinks she'd be better off if they went  their separate ways in the party  universe joy plans to drive away from  the laundry mats and never look back joy  has a crushing fear of inadequacy she  doesn't just believe that she'll never  be enough for her mother she also feels  that she could never matter at all in a  final effort to calm that fear evelyn  confesses that yes the multiverse is  unfathomably large but out of the  limitless possibilities of existence  what she wants most is to be with her  daughter it doesn't matter that there  are other better versions of each of  them out there the lives they're living  right now are just as insignificant and  therefore just as important as any of  the others because we've traveled across  the multiverse we found each other yeah  that was one thing we discovered was  like at the end of the day we are  looking for each other  part three all at once is a brief  epilogue that takes place entirely in  the party universe evelyn is a changed  woman now more affectionate towards  weymouth more accepting of joy and becky  and sporting a more positive outlook on  life if a viewer is so inclined they  might argue that the entire adventure in  the multiverse has occurred only in  evelyn's head and that the party  universe is the only real universe in  the film as evelyn sits down with her  family for their makeup meeting with 